WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:06.560
VALLIAPPA LAKSHMANAN:
Let's talk about how

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.470
to accelerate data
powered innovation

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:11.090
within your enterprise.

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:13.040
We will look at some
of the key challenges

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.370
that enterprises face,
and a five step journey

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:19.640
that we have seen lots
of successful businesses

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:22.850
take to get to data
powered innovation.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.880
We'll look at what enterprises
encounter along the way

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.870
and discuss how a Google
Cloud can help with examples.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:30.680
Hello.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:31.990
I'm Val Lakshmanan.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:36.070
I lead Analytics and AI
Solutions at Google Cloud.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.690
What are some of
the key challenges

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:40.480
that enterprises
face, especially when

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.100
it comes to data?

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.400
Well firstly, and probably
the most important,

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.570
is the size of data.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.400
The amount of data
in most enterprises

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.940
is growing dramatically,
20% to 30% year on year,

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.610
such that, when you look
back after about five years,

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:02.430
you're looking at about a 5X
increase in the amount of data.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.710
And it's not just
the size of the data.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:10.660
By 2025, a quarter of
that data that you have

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.970
is going to be
real-time in nature.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.300
And as you know,
real-time data is harder

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:22.000
to manage and maintain
than historical data.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.120
In addition, much of the data
tends to be unstructured.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.230
And today, less than
1% of unstructured data

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:34.050
is analyzed or even used.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.990
And so these are some of
the key challenges when

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.930
it comes to data and
technology that's

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.200
used to work with the data.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.690
And then put in
place the environment

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.740
that you're in today, and
you have unprecedented market

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:50.460
shifts.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.390
And so you need to be
able to analyze the data,

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:57.600
process the data, increase
the velocity and scale,

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.230
if you want to power
data powered innovation

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:02.700
through your data.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.120
And so, lots of
enterprises are driving

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.490
their data driven
business transformations

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:09.600
using Google Cloud.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.450
So whether it's businesses
in retail or gaming

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.300
or financial services,
and all over the world,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.840
whether it's in the Americas
or India or Japan and APAC,

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.180
we have experience
helping leaders

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.640
in all of these companies
bring about data-driven change.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.040
And this consists of
a five step process.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.030
And the first step is
to stop the bleeding.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:33.610
You need to reduce the
cost of managing data,

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:37.080
especially as the data
sizes grow dramatically.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.580
And one of the ways
that you do that

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.680
is by migrating the
data lake and data

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.870
warehouse to the Cloud.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.210
Once you have your
data centralized,

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:49.020
the next bit is to break down
silos within the organization,

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:53.040
build a data culture to
democratize analytics.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.700
And once you have everyone
within your organization

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:00.450
able to access the
data, process it,

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.550
the third stage
of the journey is

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:07.080
to be able to make decisions in
context faster and real time.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.450
And I'll talk about what
I mean by in context.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:12.300
But once you're in this
place where you're basically

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.800
doing descriptive analytics
as well as possible,

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.430
it is then a time to move on
to predictive and prescriptive

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:21.390
analytics.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.300
And at that point, a
first step in the journey

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.090
is to look at end-to-end
packaged AI solutions

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.120
to leapfrog so that you're
not building everything

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.132
from scratch.

00:03:31.132 --> 00:03:33.090
You're able to take
advantage of what's already

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:34.750
available in the marketplace.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:36.960
And finally, to
be able to empower

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.450
data powered innovation
that is truly

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:43.110
differentiated by building
and operationalizing AI.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.210
So let's walk through
each of these steps

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:47.910
and talk about what
enterprises encounter

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:51.140
along the way and some of the
things that we have to do.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:55.310
The first step, as I said, is
to find the budget to carry out

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:56.810
data powered innovation.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:58.490
And in order to
do that, you have

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:00.470
to lower the cost
of ownership, you

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:02.490
have to simplify operations.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:06.610
And so that is the first stage
on the journey to carrying out

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:08.570
data innovation.

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:11.300
The problem is that your
traditional enterprise data

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:15.680
warehouse implementations, they
hinder business transformation.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.930
First of all, they
can't scale fast enough

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:22.230
to keep up with this 5X growth
in data that we talked about.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:26.900
And secondly, as 25% of the
data becomes streamed in nature,

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:30.200
it becomes really important
that all of your queries

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:31.880
are on fresh data.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.040
And you need your
data to be fresh.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:38.210
And that is a problem with a
lot of enterprise data warehouse

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:39.440
implementations.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.570
Thirdly, when we talk
about breaking down

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:43.910
silos and building
a data culture,

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.340
it is extremely
important to make sure

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:48.980
that you do that
in a secure way.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:52.490
And oftentimes, the way
that security is done

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:54.740
is it provides data
access restrictions that

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.170
inhibit collaboration
between different parts

00:04:57.170 --> 00:04:58.250
of your enterprise.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:02.080
And of course, the cost of
maintaining-- and by cost,

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:04.850
we don't just mean the
software costs or license

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:08.790
costs, but also the cost
of operationalizing it.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.390
So you're basically
spending a lot of time

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:14.970
tuning the data warehouse rather
than getting insights from it.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:18.590
And finally, we want to make
ourselves ready to carry out

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:20.170
machine learning and AI.

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:23.330
And that is also something
that traditional [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.760
implementations kind of hinder.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:29.120
So that's the reason why
a lot of industry leaders

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:30.800
rely on Google
Cloud when it comes

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:32.460
to modernizing their data.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:33.890
Whether it's Home
Depot who wanted

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:35.920
to accelerate their
time to value;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:40.040
or Zulily, who needed to be able
to get instant insights for all

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.920
of their offers that
they create dynamically;

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:45.860
or Hearst Newspapers
wanted to use

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:48.230
advanced predictive
and analytics AI;

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:50.960
or AccuWeather, which is
looking at sharing data

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:52.730
not just within
their organization,

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:55.730
but with all of their partner
organizations, and to share

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.820
and have those kinds
of insights with ease.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:02.685
And doing all of this in
a secure way, which like,

00:06:02.685 --> 00:06:04.810
you know, you want to be
able to protect your data,

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:07.520
operate it with
trust, as HSBC does.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:10.490
The technological
capabilities of Google Cloud,

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.040
the fact that our data
warehouse is serverless,

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:17.240
it's self tuning, that we offer
you high performance streaming

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.360
ingestion, that every SQL
query is automatically

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:23.060
streaming in nature, that you
can operationalize machine

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:25.370
learning without ever
moving your data out

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:26.760
of the data warehouse.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.960
This is what allows you
to modernize your data

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:31.710
architecture.

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.980
And of course, all of this
is set up from the premise

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:38.730
that you need to find budget to
carry out your data innovation.

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:41.370
And that budget,
you're going to get.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:44.070
Because by migrating
to the Cloud,

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.740
we can lower your
total cost of ownership

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:52.510
massively, so oftentimes, more
than a 50% reduction in cost.

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:55.240
But it's not just about
the data warehouse.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:59.650
Because data platforms and
data rules are converging,

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:01.600
and they need to interoperate.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:04.110
So when we talk about
a data warehouse,

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:08.740
a data warehouse today functions
often as a data lake also.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:11.230
So, where we
traditionally used to have

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:13.780
extract, transform,
and load, these days,

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:17.000
you just extract and load
directly into a data warehouse.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:20.060
And from there, you
transform as necessary.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:22.960
And if you have Hadoop
workloads in your data lake,

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.510
you want those Hadoop workloads
to be able to read data

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:26.950
from your data warehouse.

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:28.210
And vise versa.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:30.850
You might have queries
in your data warehouse

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:33.370
doing a Federated query
on your data link.

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:36.350
So, your data lake and
your data warehouse

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:40.930
need to be interoperable and
form the common data platform.

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:43.180
And this is true not
just to the technology.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:46.000
It is also true from the
perspective of the people who

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.480
are going to be using the data.

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:50.830
So whether it is data analysts
using business intelligence

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:54.750
tools, or data scientists
using a data science platform,

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:57.730
or machine learning scientists
and data scientists using

00:07:57.730 --> 00:08:00.310
TensorFlow and machine
learning, they all

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:03.900
need to be able to access data
whether it is in the data lake

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.620
or whether it's in
the data warehouse.

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:07.720
That interoperability
and this ability

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:11.110
to build a data platform
that combines both the data

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:13.870
warehouse and the data lake
are extremely important.

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:16.740
And that is the way they've been
designed Google Cloud's data

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:17.630
platform.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:20.050
So, our idea is
that you need to be

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:23.440
able to derive a return on
investment from your data lake

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:27.350
so that it's a business asset,
and not just an IT cost.

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:29.710
So all of the things that
I've talked about in terms

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:32.140
of modernizing your
data warehouse,

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:33.909
they apply to a
data lake as well.

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:36.190
You want to get large
scale performance.

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:37.990
You want to do this
at a lower cost.

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:41.080
You want to be able to reduce
your operational complexity.

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:43.370
You definitely want to
make sure that you're

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:45.520
protecting your data,
that it's secure,

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:48.340
that you're breaking down data
silos between different parts

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:50.200
of your organization,
and they're

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:55.250
using AI to extract value from
all of your unstructured data.

00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:58.030
So whether it's Pandora or
the Broad Institute or Metro,

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:00.460
all of these people
are building data lakes

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:03.730
for their unstructured data,
for their semi structured data,

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:06.610
and for their Hadoop
workloads on Google Cloud

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:09.160
such that it is
a business asset.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:12.190
And for example,
dunnhumby had a blog

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:13.930
where they talked
about how they were

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:18.080
able to move from their
traditional monolithic Hadoop

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:20.930
cluster to ephemeral
clusters-- by ephemeral,

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:23.980
we mean a cluster
that is job specific--

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:26.980
and get a cost
savings of over 75%.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:29.642
So again, the idea of
moving your data lake

00:09:29.642 --> 00:09:31.350
into data warehouse,
the reason that it's

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:33.760
the first stage
in your journey is

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:36.220
that it opens up
the budget to do

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:38.840
all of the rest of the
innovation that you want to do.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:40.640
And in order to
help you do that,

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:43.960
we have a lower risk offer
to migrate and modernize

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:46.920
your enterprise data
warehouses and your data lakes.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:48.880
So if it's a data
warehouse, we have

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:52.640
ways to virtualize and translate
your queries automatically

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:54.490
and optimize and
reduce your cost.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:56.440
If it's a data
lake, we have ways

00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:00.490
to lift and shift, and then
move towards ephemeral clusters,

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:04.413
and then moving into a
completely serverless system.

00:10:04.413 --> 00:10:06.830
Once you have moved your data
warehouse and your data lake

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:09.670
to the Cloud, the
next step is to look

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:11.950
at how to democratize
data access

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:15.920
and build a data culture
within your organization.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:18.940
To talk about the
kind of benefits

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:21.730
that you get, take,
for example, HSBC

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:23.590
where they had to
calculate the liquidity

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:25.330
position of a country.

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:27.600
It used to take
them 10 hours to do.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.760
But they were able to do
it in less than 30 minutes.

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:33.370
Then they were able to do
financial crime analytics

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:35.620
using BigQuery 10 times faster.

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:38.050
But the reason they
were able to do all this

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:41.740
was because they were able to
move their workloads to GCP

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:44.200
such that all of their
teams around the world

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:47.320
had access to capabilities
to help them innovate.

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:49.670
So we see the result
of the innovation.

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:51.790
But the reason that
that is possible

00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:54.640
is because of this
democratization,

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:57.460
and of the breaking
down of data sites.

00:10:57.460 --> 00:11:00.310
And if you're a
company that need

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:02.580
to move your data
to the Cloud, you

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:05.530
want to make sure that you're
moving it in such a way

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:08.590
and breaking down your data
silos in such a way that you

00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:12.380
have strong security
and governance controls.

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:12.880
Right?

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:15.010
Especially when we
talk about, well, we

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:17.020
want to basically
provide access to data

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:21.010
from any team who can access
it who's allowed to access it.

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:22.900
You want to make sure
that all of your data

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:25.120
is encrypted in
transit, in rest.

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:28.570
There's not a bucket out there
that's somehow left open,

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:31.930
that you're able to bring
in your own encryption keys,

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:35.650
that you're able to use
Cloud DLP for classifying

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:37.240
and redacting sensitive data.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:40.510
You're able to discover
data, that all of your data

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:42.160
that you're putting
onto the Cloud

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:45.310
is subject to third party
audits, and certifications.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:47.380
If you need to be HIPPA
compliant, that you

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:48.970
can be HIPPA compliant.

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:52.050
And finally, that if
someone accesses the data,

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:54.460
there is access transparency
so that you can go look

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:56.500
at the audit logs
and you know who

00:11:56.500 --> 00:11:58.700
has accessed it, when
they accessed it,

00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:01.110
and why they accessed it.

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:02.840
So when you're
moving data and you

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:04.970
want to make sure
that your data is

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:08.780
available to a broad set
of your organization, one

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:13.100
of the key problems is, how
do we know what data I have?

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:15.410
And that's where Data
Catalog comes in.

00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:18.650
We have a simplified data
discovery mechanism such

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:21.980
that you can go ahead and
find what data sets you have.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:25.410
And Data Catalog has built-in
governance, that you can,

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:30.260
for example, say, you
can tag pieces of data--

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:33.470
not necessarily entire cables,
not necessarily entire data

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:34.010
sets.

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:38.600
But just columns, and say, these
columns are sensitive data.

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:40.730
And you can govern
sensitive data

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:45.590
across the organization in
a simple, straightforward,

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:47.890
consistent way.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:51.980
But, one of the things
that happens is,

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:55.260
as you build your data
warehouse on the Cloud,

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:56.870
you start to
centralize your data.

00:12:56.870 --> 00:13:00.120
You bring your data from SAP,
from Teradata, from Oracle,

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:02.040
from YouTube, from Google Ads.

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:04.290
And you centralize them
in the data warehouse.

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:06.990
The data warehouse on Google
Cloud is called BigQuery.

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:11.670
Once it's centralized there,
what many organizations find

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:15.090
is that they start to
see analytic silos.

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:18.390
So rather than carry out the
analysis in the data warehouse

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:21.810
itself, you see people
taking extracts of the data,

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:25.940
throwing it into spreadsheets,
throwing it into dashboards.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:29.530
And all of these things start
to become slightly out of date.

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:32.460
So the next step
is to look at, how

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:34.860
can we do analytics
in a modern way?

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:38.760
And the way to do that is to
apply governance and control

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:40.260
to your KPIs.

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:43.410
And so, we're looking
at modern [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:44.910
and analytics such
that you can have

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:47.040
dashboards with
titles that are shared

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:48.540
across your organization.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:51.720
Reports and
self-service, definitely.

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:54.990
But all of their self-service
reports and exploration

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:58.530
take advantage of
centrally built KPIs.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:01.710
And this allows you
to take insights

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:05.640
and embed them into websites
and existing applications.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:09.120
And also build data-driven
workflows and custom

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:10.290
applications with it.

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:13.295
So, it's one thing to
centralize your data.

00:14:13.295 --> 00:14:14.670
But you also need
to look at, how

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:16.920
do I centralize the
analytics on the data?

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:20.490
How do I make sure that
those analytics themselves

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:23.130
are reusable instead
of everybody writing

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:26.860
their own Excel formulas, have
everybody writing their own SQL

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:28.500
queries.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:32.030
And this is, again, something
that a lot of our customers

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:35.330
have found great benefit and
Credit Karma, for example,

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:38.070
says, there used to be a lot
of excel spreadsheets floating

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:38.570
around.

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:42.380
And now it's all in Looker
and under common governance.

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:45.620
Or Auto Trader talking
about democratization

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:47.930
of how they were, in a
matter of weeks, able

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:50.110
to go from nothing
to something that

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:52.410
was usable by all of
their sales squads.

00:14:55.520 --> 00:14:58.190
So the first step is to
move your data to the Cloud.

00:14:58.190 --> 00:15:02.090
Second step is to open up
access and build a data culture.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:05.390
And the third step,
now, now everyone

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:07.400
has access to the
data, the third step

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:11.540
is to be able to make
decisions in context faster

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:13.350
and with real-time AI.

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:16.790
So one of the key things
when we talk about context

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:20.300
is that location is
incredibly important.

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:24.020
When we look at tabular
data from most enterprises,

00:15:24.020 --> 00:15:27.080
we see that about
15% of tables tend

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:28.940
to have location
information in there--

00:15:28.940 --> 00:15:32.420
store locations, customer
information, et cetera.

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:36.080
And you would need to be able to
do an accurate spatial analysis

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:37.440
within your data warehouse.

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:40.070
You don't want have a
separate geospatial database.

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:42.070
And that's one of the key
things that you can do

00:15:42.070 --> 00:15:46.340
is that we can analyze GIS
data with familiar SQL, part

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:49.820
of your dashboards, and be able
to incorporate your location

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:54.690
information because location is
incredibly important context.

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:58.460
The second big piece
of context is time.

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:03.210
So, real-time data is
very, very, very valuable.

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:07.040
So there is a big
difference between being

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:10.970
able to act on a loan
three days later,

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:13.880
or being able to make
a decision on a loan

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:17.480
within a matter of minutes,
or within a matter of seconds.

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:19.670
Real-time is real value.

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:22.940
And whether it's in e-commerce,
or in gaming or manufacturing,

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:26.060
or in retail, you want to
be able to make decisions

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:28.370
as quickly as possible.

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:30.510
And in order to
do that, again, we

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:32.590
start with the data warehouse.

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:35.780
But the key thing to realize
is that in the data warehouse

00:16:35.780 --> 00:16:40.500
on Google Cloud, every SQL
query is automatically streamed.

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:42.150
So all of the
investments that you

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:46.290
have done to get to the
point of democratizing access

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:50.790
and building dashboards and
building common analytics, all

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:53.850
of these actually work
in real-time as well.

00:16:53.850 --> 00:16:55.740
What we need to
do is to make sure

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:57.390
that you're ingesting,
transforming,

00:16:57.390 --> 00:16:59.840
and enriching the
data, and streaming it

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:01.260
into the data warehouse.

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:04.515
And that's something that we
do naturally in Google Cloud

00:17:04.515 --> 00:17:08.079
because both Pub/Sub and
Dataflow, just like BigQuery,

00:17:08.079 --> 00:17:09.690
are all serverless.

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:13.119
They are very easy to
operate, very low cost.

00:17:13.119 --> 00:17:16.319
And you can spend
all of your time

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:20.280
getting insights from the data
rather than on operationalizing

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:24.420
and on the IT infrastructure
of building a messaging system

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:27.290
or building a data system.

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:31.060
And once you have your
data in the data warehouse,

00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:34.210
you can do machine learning
directly in the data warehouse.

00:17:34.210 --> 00:17:36.840
You don't have to move your
data out of your data warehouse.

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:39.650
And this allows you to
carry out machine learning

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:42.300
as part of your streaming
analytics by your clients,

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:44.880
for things like [INAUDIBLE]
action for example.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:47.198
And this is an
incredible benefit.

00:17:47.198 --> 00:17:49.490
Because normally, when you
talk about machine learning,

00:17:49.490 --> 00:17:51.420
you're talking
about a data science

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.050
team that has to replicate
all of this data.

00:17:54.050 --> 00:17:57.510
And whenever you replicate data,
you run into security issue,

00:17:57.510 --> 00:17:58.010
right?

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:00.970
You have two copies of
your data floating around.

00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:03.160
So BigQuery machine
learning, by allowing

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:05.160
you to do machine learning
directing your data

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:08.850
warehouse enhances
your security posture,

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:11.580
enhances your
access transparency.

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:14.940
And this is exactly what
A&amp;B Tasty, for example,

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:15.960
was able to do.

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:17.910
They have 25 user
sessions per day.

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:20.520
They need to analyze
50 billion events.

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:23.310
And they wanted to
make better decisions

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:26.430
and obtain increased revenue.

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:28.830
And in order to
do that, they were

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:32.100
able to basically personalize
access to large volumes of data

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:35.660
within their company
to basically carry out

00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:38.590
machine learning operations.

00:18:38.590 --> 00:18:41.240
So at this point,
then, in the journey,

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:44.620
you have the ability
to have a data culture,

00:18:44.620 --> 00:18:49.090
to be able to make
decisions in context--

00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:51.850
spatial context,
temporal context.

00:18:51.850 --> 00:18:54.260
And then, the next
step is to say, OK,

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:57.580
how can we now do more
prescriptive analytics?

00:18:57.580 --> 00:18:59.910
And at that point,
you want to say,

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:01.820
you look around the marketplace.

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:05.580
It's not necessarily that you
have to build every piece of AI

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:06.595
that you need.

00:19:06.595 --> 00:19:08.050
What can you buy?

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:10.630
Because when you buy,
you can leapfrog.

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:17.700
And at Google Cloud, we have
lots of readily embeddable AI

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:18.230
mod.

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:21.160
So for example, if you
want to process video,

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:23.740
and you want to do video
classification or action

00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:26.380
recognition or
object tracking, you

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:28.660
don't need to go build
this from scratch.

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:30.920
We have an API that you can use.

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:35.220
And you can use it directly
and in your applications.

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:37.490
For example, if you want
to do a translation,

00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:40.150
you don't need to go build
your own translation module.

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:44.350
Bloomberg, for example, every
time a news article comes in,

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:48.880
they need to send a global
update in 40 languages

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:50.770
to over 170 countries.

00:19:50.770 --> 00:19:53.180
And they do that through
Google Translate.

00:19:53.180 --> 00:19:55.620
And so the idea here
is that Bloomberg

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:58.320
is quite capable of building
their own translation model.

00:19:58.320 --> 00:19:59.350
But they didn't.

00:19:59.350 --> 00:20:01.600
Instead, what they did
was that they bought it.

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:03.180
And similarly,
right, if you want

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:06.300
to improve the performance
of your contact center,

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:08.790
there are three things that
you want to be able to do.

00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:11.370
You want to be able to
look at standard queries

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:13.780
and automate them
as much as possible.

00:20:13.780 --> 00:20:17.327
And if a query is handled
by a call center agent,

00:20:17.327 --> 00:20:19.410
you want to make sure that
that query gets handled

00:20:19.410 --> 00:20:21.000
the first time correctly.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:24.290
You want to improve the customer
service, your office support

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:26.760
organization, by
providing the call center

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:28.710
agent all of the
information that they

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:30.780
need as soon as they need it.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:34.050
And finally, you also
want to use the support

00:20:34.050 --> 00:20:36.270
calls that are coming
in to understand

00:20:36.270 --> 00:20:37.660
your business better.

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:39.540
And this is, again,
something that you

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:41.130
can build from scratch.

00:20:41.130 --> 00:20:44.190
But why do that when
you can leapfrog ahead

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:47.430
with the contact center AI
solution from Google Cloud.

00:20:47.430 --> 00:20:50.047
And that's what Ticketmaster
does, for example.

00:20:50.047 --> 00:20:51.630
Or that's what Marks
and Spencer does.

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:55.290
Where they're basically taking
advantage of our contact center

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:59.100
AI solution to improve
their customer service.

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:03.120
Similarly, if you have forms and
you want to extract information

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:05.550
from forms, you want
to do form parsing,

00:21:05.550 --> 00:21:07.530
you could build it from scratch.

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:10.140
Or you can use our
document AI intuition

00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:13.650
and leapfrog ahead and allow
us to basically carry out

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:16.860
all of the hard work of doing
natural language processing

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:19.270
and building AI, et cetera.

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:22.210
Then, for example, if you
wanted to claims processing,

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:24.940
here's an insurance company that
was able to use our document

00:21:24.940 --> 00:21:28.480
AI to get an incredible
reduction in the claims

00:21:28.480 --> 00:21:32.710
processing and settlement
time by, again, leapfrogging.

00:21:32.710 --> 00:21:35.440
So the key thing with
AI is to think about

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:37.450
what kinds of things
do I need to build

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:39.400
and what kinds of
things can I buy?

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:42.560
And when it comes
to buying AI models,

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:46.240
you want to buy AI models
that are high quality, that

00:21:46.240 --> 00:21:49.240
have been battle tested.

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:52.210
And that's something that you
can get out of our platform.

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:55.090
The fifth step,
then, at this point,

00:21:55.090 --> 00:21:57.100
you have prescriptive analytics.

00:21:57.100 --> 00:21:59.050
But the prescriptive
analytics that you have

00:21:59.050 --> 00:22:01.480
is primarily things that
you are able to buy.

00:22:01.480 --> 00:22:06.760
The last step of the journey to
doing data powered innovation

00:22:06.760 --> 00:22:10.270
is to empower your data
and machine learning teams,

00:22:10.270 --> 00:22:13.430
and give them the tools
for them to be successful.

00:22:13.430 --> 00:22:16.840
So, let's step back a
bit and think about,

00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:19.060
I talked about
leapfrogging with AI.

00:22:19.060 --> 00:22:21.100
And now I'm talking
about custom AI.

00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:24.950
And the idea is that
in your organization,

00:22:24.950 --> 00:22:28.060
you're going to have a
portfolio of business use cases.

00:22:28.060 --> 00:22:31.210
Not everything needs to
be built from scratch.

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:33.580
And not everything
can be bought.

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:36.520
So, you need to look
at your use cases

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:40.600
and make a determination
of those use cases

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:42.130
on three criteria.

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:43.810
How fast do you want it?

00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:45.970
How much effort do
you want to put in?

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:48.910
And how much differentiation
does this business

00:22:48.910 --> 00:22:50.200
use case offer?

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:53.920
And based on that,
then you choose

00:22:53.920 --> 00:22:57.850
whether you want to use a
fully prepackaged AI model.

00:22:57.850 --> 00:23:00.850
Or do you want to take a
bunch of building blocks

00:23:00.850 --> 00:23:03.490
and combine them
in a bespoke way?

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:06.190
Or do you want to
build custom AI that

00:23:06.190 --> 00:23:08.260
is fully, fully differentiated?

00:23:08.260 --> 00:23:11.437
And regardless of which
of these you want to do,

00:23:11.437 --> 00:23:13.270
we have a platform that
allows you to do it,

00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:15.250
that gives you the
ability to do it.

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:17.560
And we can partner
with you to help you

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:20.600
on this journey of whether it's
using the AI out of the box,

00:23:20.600 --> 00:23:25.160
or building up on top
of our building blocks,

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:29.570
like auto ML and BigQuery,
and the Vision API, et cetera,

00:23:29.570 --> 00:23:31.750
or whether you want
to do custom AI.

00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:35.560
But the idea here is that you
will have lots of applications

00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:38.300
that are collecting and
storing data into the data lake

00:23:38.300 --> 00:23:39.550
and data warehouse.

00:23:39.550 --> 00:23:42.250
That is the first part
of your journey to doing

00:23:42.250 --> 00:23:43.690
data powered innovation.

00:23:43.690 --> 00:23:47.320
And having done that,
then you basically

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:52.160
arm your data science teams to
be able to build and use AI,

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:54.960
and build and use AI
regardless of whether it

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.970
is AI out of the box or
building blocks or custom AI.

00:23:57.970 --> 00:24:00.610
Being able to take it,
experiment with it,

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:02.467
generate insights from it.

00:24:02.467 --> 00:24:05.050
But once you start to see that
this data science team is being

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:07.420
successful in the
developments phase,

00:24:07.420 --> 00:24:10.720
you need to have the ability to
promote it and operationalize

00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:11.880
the machine learning.

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:13.870
And that's where
we call MLOps to be

00:24:13.870 --> 00:24:17.680
able to do this routinely, to
be able to monitor and retrain

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:21.010
the model when the
models start going out

00:24:21.010 --> 00:24:25.650
of sync because of changes in
the environment of the data.

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:30.470
And, for example, eBay, for
example, uses custom AI.

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:34.030
Third part of my model
where they wanted

00:24:34.030 --> 00:24:36.250
to do visual product search.

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:38.050
And at that point,
the training and image

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:41.190
classifier on 55
million product images

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:45.280
to get a 10% increase in
image recognition accuracy.

00:24:45.280 --> 00:24:49.580
And 10% is an incredible
increase in accuracy.

00:24:49.580 --> 00:24:52.690
But able to do that
10 times faster

00:24:52.690 --> 00:24:56.050
because of the amazing
hardware called TPUs

00:24:56.050 --> 00:24:57.900
that are available on Cloud.

00:24:57.900 --> 00:25:01.390
Again, Google Cloud, incredibly
differentiated in terms

00:25:01.390 --> 00:25:06.500
of both our data platform and
in terms of our AI platform.

00:25:06.500 --> 00:25:10.480
So, when you are
taking your journey

00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:13.520
to enable data
powered innovation

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:17.680
within your organization,
we can help you derive value

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:20.110
at every step of the
journey, whether it's

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:23.050
to reduce TCO in the
first stage, whether it's

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:25.910
to democratize analytics
in the second stage,

00:25:25.910 --> 00:25:28.630
whether it's to be able
to make higher value

00:25:28.630 --> 00:25:31.240
decisions in the third
stage, whether it's being

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:34.570
able to leapfrog
your competition

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:37.720
in the fourth stage, or
being able to really create

00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:41.860
differentiated transformative
